{
  // Plan Decision 10: **a new Worker of our own, outside `spike/`.**
  //
  // There is exactly one other Worker config in this repo — `spike/wrangler.jsonc`,
  // `name: laneways-spike` — and it is still deployed, still serving the M0
  // measurement harness, and still accepting `POST /api/result` into a live D1
  // database. The Global Constraints forbid touching `spike/`, so "modify the
  // existing Worker config" was either forbidden by this plan's own rule or a
  // repoint of a deployed artefact somebody is still collecting data from.
  //
  // This one is a **different `name`**, which is what makes it a different
  // Worker: `wrangler deploy` keys on it. `laneways-spike` stays where it is.
  "name": "laneways",

  // **No `main`, and that is the deliberate difference from the spike.** M2 is a
  // static-asset deploy: there is no server-side code, no route handler and
  // nothing to store until M3's persistence. Adding `main` here would also mean
  // adding an `assets.binding`, which only exists so a Worker script can serve
  // assets itself — and the whole point is that no script runs.
  //
  // **No `d1_databases`, for the same reason and one more.** The spike's binding
  // names a real database with real rows in it; declaring the same binding here
  // would put a second Worker on it, and M2 submits nothing to a leaderboard
  // anyway (the starting city is out of band, so an M2 run is not replayable —
  // see `src/startingCity.ts`).
  "compatibility_date": "2026-08-06",

  "assets": {
    // Vite's `build.outDir`. `public/_headers` is copied in here verbatim and is
    // what Cloudflare's static-asset server reads for the `no-store` rule on
    // `/` and `/index.html` (spec §8.5).
    "directory": "./dist"
  },

  "observability": {
    "enabled": true
  }
}
